#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
Purpose: Executable reference implementation of the `/ds-cleanup-worktrees`
         predicate (DS-worktree-reaper). The command file's Steps 2-4 have
         always been prose an operator must read and execute by hand;
         nothing in the codebase ever invoked it automatically, which is
         the single largest confirmed cause of worktree accumulation (33
         non-root worktrees measured in this repo alone, oldest from
         2026-07-09; see the ticket brief for the full diagnosis). This
         tool makes that predicate a real, machine-invocable command so it
         can be wired into `bin/ds-base-sync` (advisory dry-run) and a
         SessionStart nudge, in addition to remaining operator-invocable.

         Classification reuses `bin/tests/worktree_model.py`'s
         `classify_entry` (path-prefix classification: `.claude/worktrees/`
         = ISOLATION, `.agentic/worktrees/` = CONDUCTOR_CREATED, anything
         else = UNMANAGED) - the single normative definition, never
         restated here. `evals/.worktrees/*` (pinned per repo decision
         #203) already classifies UNMANAGED under that model and is never
         touched by this tool regardless.

         Removal predicate (worktree REMOVAL only - `git worktree remove`,
         never `git branch -D`; a worktree's branch and its commits
         survive removal, matching the existing rationale in
         `disposition_for`'s own docstring: "git worktree remove does not
         destroy commits", used to justify treating a bare MERGED PR as
         sufficient there). A worktree is removable only when ALL of:
           1. its working tree is clean (no uncommitted or untracked
              changes) - checked with `git status --porcelain`;
           2. it is not currently locked by a running agent OR is locked
              but the directory is confirmed reclaimable per the harness's
              own lock-while-running contract (see the Locked handling
              note below);
           3. its branch is provably resolved via ANY of, first match
              wins:
                a. branch is gone from origin (`git ls-remote --heads
                   origin <branch>` returns no match) - safe here
                   specifically BECAUSE this tool only ever removes the
                   WORKTREE, never the branch or its commits; a
                   never-pushed branch's local ref and reflog remain
                   fully intact after worktree removal, so this leg
                   cannot destroy anything a stricter, branch-deletion-
                   grade predicate (bin/ds-branch-prune, DS-153) would
                   refuse - it merely reclaims the checkout directory.
                b. the branch's PR state (via `gh`) is MERGED or CLOSED -
                   deliberately more permissive than `disposition_for`'s
                   shared MERGED-only leg (see worktree_model.py), again
                   because worktree removal is non-destructive of commits
                   and a CLOSED (abandoned) PR means the operator already
                   decided the branch's fate.
                c. the branch is an ancestor of the base ref
                   (`origin/main` by default).
         Any leg failing (or all three inconclusive) reports the entry
         rather than removing it - never a force-delete on an inconclusive
         read.

         Anything failing leg 1 or 2 is ALWAYS reported, never removed,
         regardless of what leg 3 would say - a dirty worktree is never
         touched by this tool under any circumstance.

Public API (CLI):
  ds-reap-worktrees [--repo <path>] [--base <ref>] [--dry-run] [--no-gh]
                    [--explain]

  --repo <path>     Repository to operate on. Default: cwd.
  --base <ref>      Base ref to prove ancestor-of-base evidence against.
                     Default: "origin/main".
  --dry-run         Compute and report; remove nothing.
  --no-gh           Force degraded mode (skip the PR-state leg) without
                     attempting to invoke `gh` at all.
  --explain         Print one line per worktree entry with its outcome
                     (default: aggregate counts only).

Locked handling: the live Claude Code harness locks every isolation
worktree at creation (`locked claude agent agent-<id> ...`) and a plain
`git worktree remove` already refuses a locked entry. Per
`content/references/worktree-lifecycle.md` §Guardrail, `git worktree
unlock` may be used ONLY on a worktree whose directory is already gone (an
admin-only entry - its agent cannot still be running). This tool honors
that guardrail exactly: a locked entry whose directory still exists is
ALWAYS reported (`skipped-locked`), never unlocked or force-removed,
regardless of dirty status or branch resolution - the harness's lock is
cross-session protection this tool must never override. A locked entry
whose directory is already gone is handled by the admin-prune pass
(`git worktree prune`) at the end of every run, same as an unlocked
directory-missing entry - `git worktree unlock` fires first only for that
directory-missing case, exactly mirroring the session-start prune script's
own unlock-then-prune pattern in worktree-lifecycle.md.

Upstream deps: Python 3 stdlib only (argparse, shutil, subprocess, sys,
               pathlib, collections). `git` (CLI) and, unless --no-gh,
               `gh` (CLI, for the PR-state leg - degrades gracefully when
               absent or unauthenticated). bin/tests/worktree_model.py
               (WorktreeClass, classify_entry, parse_porcelain, imported
               by path exactly like bin/ds-branch-prune does, so a
               PATH-symlink invocation still finds it).

Downstream consumers: content/commands/ds-cleanup-worktrees.md (this tool
                      is the executable form of that command's Steps 2-4
                      predicate; the command file remains the
                      operator-readable prose entry point);
                      bin/ds-base-sync (advisory --dry-run report appended
                      to its tail, non-blocking, never mutates - the one
                      guaranteed post-merge trigger point);
                      hooks/session-start-wrap.sh (a non-blocking advisory
                      nudge line when the non-root worktree count exceeds
                      a small threshold, pointing at
                      `/ds-cleanup-worktrees`; report-only, never removes
                      anything from that call site).
                      CI: bin/tests/test_reap_worktrees.py (pytest,
                      auto-collected by `python3 -m pytest bin/tests/`).

Failure modes: exits 0 on success INCLUDING partial degradation (gh
               unavailable/unauthenticated/erroring/--no-gh; individual
               `git worktree remove` failures are reported to stderr and
               the run continues). Exits 1 ONLY on an internal/usage
               error: --repo is not a directory or not a git repository,
               or an unhandled exception while enumerating worktrees.
               Never removes a dirty worktree under any circumstance.
               Never removes `evals/.worktrees/*` - those classify
               UNMANAGED under `classify_entry` and are always reported,
               never touched, same as any other UNMANAGED entry.

Performance: one `git worktree list --porcelain` call, a `git status
             --porcelain` + `git ls-remote` + (optionally) one `gh pr
             view` + one `git merge-base --is-ancestor` per managed
             worktree entry, plus a final `git worktree prune`. Not
             designed for repositories with very large worktree counts.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import shutil
import subprocess
import sys
from collections import Counter
from pathlib import Path
from typing import List, Optional, Tuple

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Shared helpers: worktree_model.py (bin/tests/), imported by resolved path
# so a bare PATH-symlink invocation (~/.local/bin/ds-reap-worktrees ->
# repo bin/ds-reap-worktrees) still finds it - .resolve() follows the
# symlink back to the real bin/ directory, matching the pattern established
# in bin/ds-branch-prune and bin/ds-feedback.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
_BIN_DIR = Path(__file__).resolve().parent
sys.path.insert(0, str(_BIN_DIR / "tests"))

from worktree_model import (  # noqa: E402
    WorktreeClass,
    classify_entry,
    parse_porcelain,
)


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# git/gh plumbing
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------


def _run(args: List[str], cwd: Optional[str] = None) -> subprocess.CompletedProcess:
    return subprocess.run(args, cwd=cwd, capture_output=True, text=True)


def _resolve_repo(path: str) -> str:
    p = Path(path).expanduser()
    if not p.is_dir():
        raise ValueError(f"not a directory: {path}")
    proc = _run(["git", "-C", str(p), "rev-parse", "--show-toplevel"])
    if proc.returncode != 0:
        raise ValueError(f"not a git repository: {path}")
    return proc.stdout.strip()


def _worktree_list(repo: str) -> str:
    proc = _run(["git", "-C", repo, "worktree", "list", "--porcelain"])
    if proc.returncode != 0:
        raise RuntimeError(f"git worktree list failed: {proc.stderr.strip()}")
    return proc.stdout


def _is_dirty(worktree_path: str) -> bool:
    proc = _run(["git", "-C", worktree_path, "status", "--porcelain"])
    if proc.returncode != 0:
        # Cannot determine status - fail closed (treat as dirty, never remove).
        return True
    return bool(proc.stdout.strip())


def _ls_remote_status(repo: str, branch: str) -> str:
    """Returns "pushed" | "not_pushed" | "error"."""
    proc = _run(["git", "-C", repo, "ls-remote", "--exit-code", "--heads", "origin", branch])
    if proc.returncode == 0:
        return "pushed"
    if proc.returncode == 2:
        return "not_pushed"
    return "error"


def _is_ancestor(repo: str, ancestor: str, descendant: str) -> bool:
    proc = _run(["git", "-C", repo, "merge-base", "--is-ancestor", ancestor, descendant])
    return proc.returncode == 0


def _gh_available(repo: str, no_gh: bool) -> bool:
    if no_gh or shutil.which("gh") is None:
        return False
    proc = _run(["gh", "auth", "status"], cwd=repo)
    return proc.returncode == 0


def _pr_state(repo: str, branch: str) -> Optional[str]:
    """Returns "OPEN" | "MERGED" | "CLOSED" | None (no PR / gh error)."""
    proc = _run(["gh", "pr", "view", branch, "--json", "state", "-q", ".state"], cwd=repo)
    if proc.returncode != 0:
        return None
    val = proc.stdout.strip()
    return val or None


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Branch-resolution predicate (worktree-removal grade - see module
# docstring for why this is deliberately more permissive than the
# shared bin/tests/worktree_model.py disposition_for/_orphan_branch model)
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------


def resolve_branch(
    repo: str, branch: str, base_ref: str, gh_ok: bool
) -> Tuple[bool, str]:
    """Returns (resolved, reason). `resolved=True` means the worktree's
    branch is provably safe to reclaim the WORKTREE for (never the branch
    itself - this tool never deletes a branch).
    """
    ls = _ls_remote_status(repo, branch)
    if ls == "not_pushed":
        return True, "branch-gone-from-origin"

    pr_state = _pr_state(repo, branch) if gh_ok else None
    if pr_state == "OPEN":
        return False, "pr-open"
    if pr_state in ("MERGED", "CLOSED"):
        return True, f"pr-{pr_state.lower()}"

    if _is_ancestor(repo, branch, base_ref):
        return True, "ancestor-of-base"

    if ls == "error":
        return False, "ls-remote-error"
    return False, "unproven"


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Per-entry evaluation
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------


def evaluate_entry(repo: str, entry, wt_class: WorktreeClass, base_ref: str, gh_ok: bool) -> dict:
    """Resolves one worktree entry, returning a dict with at least `path`,
    `branch`, `class`, and `outcome`. `outcome` is one of REMOVE,
    SKIP_UNMANAGED, SKIP_DIRTY, SKIP_LOCKED, SKIP_MISSING_LOCKED,
    SKIP_UNPROVEN, SKIP_DETACHED.
    """
    if wt_class is WorktreeClass.UNMANAGED:
        return {"path": entry.path, "branch": entry.branch, "class": wt_class.value, "outcome": "SKIP_UNMANAGED"}

    exists = Path(entry.path).is_dir()

    if not exists:
        # Admin-only entry (directory already gone). Unlock if locked (the
        # ONLY case the guardrail permits unlocking - its agent cannot
        # still be running when the directory is gone) so `git worktree
        # prune` at the end can actually reclaim it.
        if entry.locked:
            return {
                "path": entry.path,
                "branch": entry.branch,
                "class": wt_class.value,
                "outcome": "SKIP_MISSING_LOCKED",
            }
        return {"path": entry.path, "branch": entry.branch, "class": wt_class.value, "outcome": "SKIP_MISSING"}

    if entry.locked:
        # Guardrail: never unlock or force-remove a worktree whose
        # directory still exists - its agent may still be running.
        return {"path": entry.path, "branch": entry.branch, "class": wt_class.value, "outcome": "SKIP_LOCKED"}

    if _is_dirty(entry.path):
        return {"path": entry.path, "branch": entry.branch, "class": wt_class.value, "outcome": "SKIP_DIRTY"}

    if entry.branch is None:
        # Detached HEAD - no branch to resolve; never remove without proof.
        return {"path": entry.path, "branch": entry.branch, "class": wt_class.value, "outcome": "SKIP_DETACHED"}

    resolved, reason = resolve_branch(repo, entry.branch, base_ref, gh_ok)
    if resolved:
        return {
            "path": entry.path,
            "branch": entry.branch,
            "class": wt_class.value,
            "outcome": "REMOVE",
            "reason": reason,
        }
    return {
        "path": entry.path,
        "branch": entry.branch,
        "class": wt_class.value,
        "outcome": "SKIP_UNPROVEN",
        "reason": reason,
    }


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CLI
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------


def parse_args(argv: Optional[List[str]] = None) -> argparse.Namespace:
    p = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        prog="ds-reap-worktrees",
        description="Executable implementation of the /ds-cleanup-worktrees removal predicate.",
    )
    p.add_argument("--repo", default=".")
    p.add_argument("--base", default="origin/main")
    p.add_argument("--dry-run", action="store_true")
    p.add_argument("--no-gh", action="store_true")
    p.add_argument("--explain", action="store_true")
    return p.parse_args(argv)


def main(argv: Optional[List[str]] = None) -> int:
    args = parse_args(argv)

    try:
        repo = _resolve_repo(args.repo)
    except ValueError as exc:
        print(f"ds-reap-worktrees: {exc}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1

    try:
        porcelain = _worktree_list(repo)
        entries = parse_porcelain(porcelain)
    except (RuntimeError, ValueError) as exc:
        print(f"ds-reap-worktrees: {exc}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    except Exception as exc:  # noqa: BLE001 - internal error, report and exit 1
        print(f"ds-reap-worktrees: internal error: {exc}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1

    if not entries:
        # Practically unreachable against a real git repo (`git worktree
        # list --porcelain` always emits at least the main worktree), but
        # the mode string is still composed from the SAME dry-run/degraded
        # axes as the populated-entries path below, not hardcoded "live" -
        # see the module docstring's --dry-run flag and the sibling
        # bin/ds-branch-prune's own documented "mode must reflect every
        # behavior-changing flag" lesson.
        empty_degraded = not _gh_available(repo, args.no_gh)
        empty_mode_parts = []
        if empty_degraded:
            empty_mode_parts.append("degraded (gh unavailable)")
        if args.dry_run:
            empty_mode_parts.append("dry-run")
        empty_mode = ", ".join(empty_mode_parts) if empty_mode_parts else "live"
        print(
            f"ds-reap-worktrees: base={args.base} mode={empty_mode} entries=0 removed=0 "
            "skipped-dirty=0 skipped-locked=0 skipped-unproven=0 pruned-admin=0"
        )
        return 0

    gh_ok = _gh_available(repo, args.no_gh)
    degraded = not gh_ok

    results = []
    for idx, entry in enumerate(entries):
        is_main = idx == 0
        wt_class = classify_entry(entry, host=repo, repo_root=repo, is_main=is_main)
        if is_main:
            results.append({"path": entry.path, "branch": entry.branch, "class": "MAIN", "outcome": "SKIP_MAIN"})
            continue
        results.append(evaluate_entry(repo, entry, wt_class, args.base, gh_ok))

    remove_results = [r for r in results if r["outcome"] == "REMOVE"]
    skip_counts = Counter(r["outcome"] for r in results if r["outcome"] != "REMOVE")

    removed = 0
    if not args.dry_run:
        for r in remove_results:
            path = r["path"]
            proc = _run(["git", "-C", repo, "worktree", "remove", path])
            if proc.returncode == 0:
                removed += 1
                print(f"REMOVED {path} (branch={r['branch']}, reason={r['reason']})")
            else:
                print(
                    f"WARNING: failed to remove {path} (git worktree remove exit "
                    f"{proc.returncode}): {proc.stderr.strip()}",
                    file=sys.stderr,
                )
    else:
        removed = len(remove_results)

    # Unlock any directory-missing locked entries so the admin-prune pass
    # below can reclaim them (the sole permitted unlock case - see the
    # Locked handling note in the module docstring).
    missing_locked = [r for r in results if r["outcome"] == "SKIP_MISSING_LOCKED"]
    if not args.dry_run:
        for r in missing_locked:
            _run(["git", "-C", repo, "worktree", "unlock", r["path"]])

    # `git worktree list --porcelain` does NOT report `prunable` for a
    # LOCKED entry whose directory is gone (git withholds prunable status
    # from a locked entry by design, even though the directory is
    # confirmed missing) - verified empirically against a scratch repo.
    # Count both directory-missing outcomes directly from `results` rather
    # than `entry.prunable`, so a missing-and-locked entry (unlocked just
    # above) is still counted here even though its ORIGINAL porcelain
    # listing never set `prunable`.
    pruned_admin = sum(1 for r in results if r["outcome"] in ("SKIP_MISSING", "SKIP_MISSING_LOCKED"))
    if not args.dry_run:
        _run(["git", "-C", repo, "worktree", "prune"])

    skipped_dirty = skip_counts.get("SKIP_DIRTY", 0)
    skipped_locked = skip_counts.get("SKIP_LOCKED", 0)
    skipped_unproven = skip_counts.get("SKIP_UNPROVEN", 0) + skip_counts.get("SKIP_DETACHED", 0)

    mode_parts = []
    if degraded:
        mode_parts.append("degraded (gh unavailable)")
    if args.dry_run:
        mode_parts.append("dry-run")
    mode = ", ".join(mode_parts) if mode_parts else "live"

    print(
        f"ds-reap-worktrees: base={args.base} mode={mode} entries={len(results)} "
        f"removed={removed} skipped-dirty={skipped_dirty} skipped-locked={skipped_locked} "
        f"skipped-unproven={skipped_unproven} pruned-admin={pruned_admin}"
    )

    if degraded:
        print(
            "NOTE: degraded mode (gh unavailable/unauthenticated/erroring/--no-gh) - only "
            "branch-gone-from-origin and ancestor-of-base evidence available for the "
            "branch-resolution leg; this can only remove FEWER worktrees than a full run, "
            "never more."
        )

    if args.explain:
        print("-- per-entry --")
        for r in results:
            reason = f" ({r['reason']})" if "reason" in r else ""
            print(f"{r['path']} [{r['class']}] branch={r['branch']}: {r['outcome']}{reason}")

    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    raise SystemExit(main())
